<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yemen</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yemen is a region in the south-west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a>, historically a centre of trade in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/incense">incense</a> and a link between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horn-of-africa">Horn of Africa</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a> extended its control over Yemen in the sixth century before being expelled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanians</a>. It was later ruled at times by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walashma-dynasty">Walashma</a> princes took refuge there before founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yemen.jpg" alt="Yemen" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 愛喝奶茶 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Yemen.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Adal Sultanate was founded around 1415 CE by Sabr ad-Din III, a prince of the Walashma dynasty who returned from exile in <b>Yemen</b> after the defeat of its predecessor state, the Ifat Sultanate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/adal-sultanate">Adal Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The loss of influence in <b>Yemen</b> to the Sasanian Persians in the 570s cut Aksum off from the eastern shore of the Red Sea and from the revenues of the trans-Arabian trade.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>He sent his brother Turanshah to conquer <b>Yemen</b> in 1174, which secured the Red Sea route, took Aleppo in 1183 and received the submission of Mosul in 1186.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ayyubid-empire">Ayyubid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The covert apparatus was the da'wa, the missionary network propagating Ismaili doctrine across Persia, Sind, <b>Yemen</b>, and Arabia under a chief missionary in Cairo.</blockquote><a href="/empire/fatimid-caliphate">Fatimid Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Himyar was a people and tribal confederation of the southern highlands of <b>Yemen</b>, south of the Kingdom of Saba, whose centre lay around Zafar, near modern Yarim.</blockquote><a href="/empire/himyarite-kingdom">Himyarite Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In the first three centuries CE Saba fought long wars with Himyar, Hadramawt and the Kingdom of Aksum, whose troops intervened in western <b>Yemen</b>, and kings such as Ilsharah Yahdub and Sha’irum Awtar recorded their campaigns in dedications at the Awwam temple.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-saba">Kingdom of Saba · Military</a></li></ul></section>
